{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DzkuRhdAE1qKDBY2ig9HRL","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd008-ls\n     2→title: \"cmd/ls: Core File Listing\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  ls is the most frequently used Unix utility, and its behavior varies between macOS BSD ls\n     6→  and GNU ls in column layout, color handling, long-format field widths, and human-readable\n     7→  size formatting. Scripts and interactive shells depend on exact output format for parsing\n     8→  and display. A Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gls,\n     9→  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag combinations, verified by\n    10→  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→  ls is the primary consumer of both pkg/sys and pkg/format. It requires terminal-width\n    13→  queries for multi-column layout, extended file metadata for long format, signal handling\n    14→  for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH, column alignment for tabular output, ANSI color for file-type\n    15→  colorization, and human-readable size conversion. This PRD covers the core flag subset\n    16→  for rel02.0; extended flags are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column, single-column, or long format, matching GNU ls output layout.\n    20→  - G2: Support filter flags (-a, -A, -d) to control which entries appear in the listing.\n    21→  - G3: Support colorized output via --color with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    22→  - G4: Support human-readable file sizes (-h) in long format, matching GNU ls -lh output.\n    23→  - G5: Produce correct exit codes (0, 1, 2) matching GNU ls exit-code contract.\n    24→\n    25→requirements:\n    26→  R1:\n    27→    title: Default listing (multi-column and single-column)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R1.1: |\n    30→          When stdout is a TTY and no format flag is given, must produce multi-column output sorted vertically (down columns, then across). Terminal width is obtained by calling pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns the current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl on stdout; returns an error when stdout is not a terminal. Entries are laid out by calling pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths.\n    31→      - R1.2: When stdout is not a TTY (pipe, file redirect) and no format flag is given, must produce single-column output with one entry per line. This matches GNU ls behavior (ls.c detects stdout is not a TTY and sets format to single-column).\n    32→      - R1.3: Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C). Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent sort divergence.\n    33→      - R1.4: Must not list entries whose names start with \".\" unless -a or -A is given. This is the default filter behavior matching GNU ls.\n    34→\n    35→  R2:\n    36→    title: Single-column mode (-1) and long format (-l)\n    37→    items:\n    38→      - R2.1: -1 must produce single-column output with one entry per line regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. This overrides the default multi-column layout.\n    39→      - R2a: |\n    40→          -l flag skeleton and permission string. When -l is given, produce long-format output. Field order (left to right): permissions (10 chars), nlink, owner, group, size, mtime, name. Fields separated by a single space. Numeric fields (nlink, size) are right-aligned in columns wide enough for the largest value in the listing.\n    41→\n    42→          Permission string algorithm (10 characters):\n    43→          - Position 0 (file type): 'd' for directory (fi.Mode&os.ModeDir != 0), 'l' for symlink (fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0), 'c' for char device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice != 0), 'b' for block device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice == 0), 'p' for FIFO (fi.Mode&os.ModeNamedPipe != 0), 's' for socket (fi.Mode&os.ModeSocket != 0), '-' for regular file.\n    44→          - Positions 1-3 (owner rwx): 'r'/'- ' for bit 0o400, 'w'/'-' for 0o200, 'x'/'-' for 0o100. If setuid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetuid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    45→          - Positions 4-6 (group rwx): bits 0o040, 0o020, 0o010. If setgid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetgid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    46→          - Positions 7-9 (other rwx): bits 0o004, 0o002, 0o001. If sticky (fi.Mode&os.ModeSticky != 0): replace 'x' with 't', or replace '-' with 'T'.\n    47→      - R2b: |\n    48→          File metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat. Obtain metadata for each listed entry by calling pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata when path is a symlink.\n    49→\n    50→          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded here for reference):\n    51→            type FileInfo struct {\n    52→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits\n    53→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    54→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    55→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    56→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    57→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    58→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    59→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    60→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    61→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    62→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n    63→                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n    64→            }\n    65→\n    66→          In long format: print fi.Nlink (uint64) right-aligned; print fi.Size (int64, bytes) right-aligned. Both columns sized to the widest value across all listed entries.\n    67→      - R2c: |\n    68→          Owner and group name resolution in long format. Resolve owner name by calling os/user.LookupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Uid))); on success use u.Username; on error (user not found or lookup fails) fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10). Resolve group name by calling os/user.LookupGroupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Gid))); on success use g.Name; on error fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10). Owner and group fields are left-padded to column width for alignment.\n    69→      - R2d: |\n    70→          Modification time formatting in long format. Use fi.ModTime (time.Time from sys.FileInfo). If time.Since(fi.ModTime) < 6*30*24*time.Hour (approximately six months), format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2 15:04\"). Otherwise format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2  2006\"). Both patterns produce a 12-character field.\n    71→      - R2e: |\n    72→          Total block count line and symlink display. Before the directory listing, print \"total N\\n\" where N = sum(fi.Blocks for all listed entries) / 2. The division converts 512-byte st_blocks units to 1K-block units matching GNU ls. N is a plain integer (no trailing suffix) unless -h is active (see R5).\n    73→\n    74→          Symlink display: when fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0, read the link target with os.Readlink(path) and append \" -> \" + target to the entry name field. Use Lstat (not Stat) so the symlink's own fi.Size and fi.Nlink are used, not the target's.\n    75→\n    76→  R3:\n    77→    title: Filter flags (-a, -A, -d)\n    78→    items:\n    79→      - R3.1: -a must include entries whose names start with \".\" including the special entries \".\" and \"..\" in the listing.\n    80→      - R3.2: -A must include entries whose names start with \".\" except for \".\" and \"..\". -A is the \"almost all\" filter.\n    81→      - R3.3: -d must list directory entries themselves rather than their contents. When a directory is given as an argument with -d, must print the directory name (or its long-format entry with -l) without descending into it.\n    82→      - R3.4: When both -a and -A are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    83→\n    84→  R4:\n    85→    title: Color output (--color)\n    86→    items:\n    87→      - R4.1: Must support --color=always, --color=auto, and --color=never. When --color is given without a value, it must default to \"always\".\n    88→      - R4.2: |\n    89→          --color=auto must colorize output only when stdout is a TTY. TTY detection: call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout) — returns true when os.Stdout's file descriptor is a terminal (detected via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with TIOCGWINSZ), false otherwise. When stdout is not a TTY, --color=auto must behave like --color=never.\n    90→      - R4.3: |\n    91→          Colorized output uses GNU LS_COLORS default color assignments. Implement color lookup directly in cmd/ls using the following table (do not call pkg/format.FileTypeColor):\n    92→\n    93→            File type         ANSI escape sequence\n    94→            ---------         --------------------\n    95→            directory         \"\\033[34m\"   (blue)\n    96→            symbolic link     \"\\033[36m\"   (cyan)\n    97→            executable file   \"\\033[32m\"   (green)\n    98→            block device      \"\\033[33;1m\" (yellow bold)\n    99→            character device  \"\\033[33;1m\" (yellow bold)\n   100→            socket            \"\\033[35m\"   (magenta)\n   101→            named pipe/FIFO   \"\\033[33m\"   (yellow)\n   102→            regular file      \"\"           (no color)\n   103→            reset sequence    \"\\033[0m\"\n   104→\n   105→          Executable detection: fi.Mode&0o111 != 0 (any execute bit set). Each entry name is wrapped as: colorCode + name + resetCode. When color is suppressed, both codes are empty strings so no ANSI sequences appear in output.\n   106→      - R4.4: When --color=never or when color is suppressed (non-TTY with auto), must not emit any ANSI escape sequences in the output.\n   107→\n   108→  R5:\n   109→    title: Human-readable sizes (-h)\n   110→    items:\n   111→      - R5.1: |\n   112→          -h must only take effect with -l (long format). When -h is given with -l, file sizes (fi.Size int64) must be displayed using the following binary HumanSize algorithm (do not call pkg/format.HumanSize):\n   113→\n   114→            func humanSize(n int64) string {\n   115→                if n == 0 { return \"0\" }\n   116→                suffixes := []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}\n   117→                f := float64(n)\n   118→                i := 0\n   119→                for f >= 1024 && i < len(suffixes)-1 { f /= 1024; i++ }\n   120→                if i == 0 { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d\", n) }\n   121→                if f == float64(int64(f)) { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d%s\", int64(f), suffixes[i]) }\n   122→                return fmt.Sprintf(\"%.1f%s\", f, suffixes[i])\n   123→            }\n   124→\n   125→          Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n   126→      - R5.2: -h must also apply to the \"total N\" block count line in long format. When -h is active, convert the total block count (sum(fi.Blocks)/2 as int64) to a human-readable string using the same binary algorithm before printing \"total X\\n\".\n   127→      - R5.3: Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n   128→\n   129→  R6:\n   130→    title: Exit codes\n   131→    items:\n   132→      - R6.1: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n   133→      - R6.2: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs, such as failing to access a file or directory given as an argument (e.g., permission denied, file not found). Must still list the remaining accessible entries before exiting. Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible entry.\n   134→      - R6.3: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs, such as an invalid command-line option. This matches GNU ls (ls.c:5638 exit(status) where status is set to EXIT_FAILURE=2 for bad options at ls.c:2026).\n   135→\n   136→  R7:\n   137→    title: Signal handling\n   138→    items:\n   139→      - R7.1: |\n   140→          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup so that piping ls output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. Implementation: call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() — this uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; a dedicated goroutine calls os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU ls behavior).\n   141→      - R7.2: Must install a SIGWINCH handler via pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that re-queries the terminal width and updates the column layout. pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize registers a SIGWINCH listener that calls pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width; multiple callbacks may be registered. This allows ls to adapt column layout if the terminal is resized during a long listing.\n   142→\n   143→non_goals:\n   144→  - -C (force multi-column), -x (sort across), -t (sort by mtime), -S (sort by size), -r (reverse sort), -U (unsorted), -v (version sort), -i (inode number), -s (block size), -n (numeric UID/GID), -R (recursive), -F (classify with indicator) are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n   145→  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) is out of scope for this project.\n   146→  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) is out of scope.\n   147→  - -Z (SELinux security context) is out of scope; this project does not target SELinux.\n   148→  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) is out of scope for this release.\n   149→  - --block-size unit suffixes beyond -h are out of scope for this release.\n   150→  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing is out of scope; we use GNU default colors only (prd003-format non-goals).\n   151→  - ACL (access control list) display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) is out of scope.\n   152→\n   153→acceptance_criteria:\n   154→  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gls and go run ./cmd/ls on a test directory produce identical multi-column output when run in a pseudo-TTY with the same terminal width. Differential test must set LC_ALL=C and --color=never.\"\n   155→  - \"AC2: Single-column comparison -- gls -1 and go run ./cmd/ls -1 produce identical output on the same test directory.\"\n   156→  - \"AC3: Long format comparison -- gls -l and go run ./cmd/ls -l produce identical output on the same test directory. Because mtime display depends on wall clock, differential tests must normalize the mtime column by replacing it with a fixed placeholder (e.g., \\\"MTIME\\\") before comparison. Because owner/group names may vary, tests must use a fixture where files are owned by the test user, or normalize owner/group fields.\"\n   157→  - \"AC4: Color suppression -- gls --color=auto and go run ./cmd/ls --color=auto produce no ANSI escape sequences when stdout is piped (not a TTY).\"\n   158→  - \"AC5: Human-readable sizes -- gls -lh and go run ./cmd/ls -lh produce identical size strings on a set of test files with known sizes (0 bytes, 1023 bytes, 1024 bytes, 1536 bytes, 1048576 bytes, 1073741824 bytes).\"\n   159→  - \"AC6: Filter flags -- gls -a, gls -A, and gls -d produce identical output to the Go binary with the same flags on a test directory containing dotfiles and subdirectories.\"\n   160→  - \"AC7: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent path argument and exits 2 when given an invalid option, matching gls behavior.\"\n   161→  - \"AC8: cmd/ls compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   162→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"449267df-e3ae-4be5-989e-1b875e969cf9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01EpoLHKWXazFvrwj8kXJyii","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LUjDUKbUEQU5Y9yQMbxXB3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5033,"cache_read_input_tokens":21912,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5033},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"760458ae-9028-422a-ab47-68183542b290"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUjDUKbUEQU5Y9yQMbxXB3","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd001-testutils\n     2→title: \"pkg/testutils: Differential Testing Harness\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Every utility in go-unix-utils is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew\n     6→  GNU reference binary side by side and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n     7→  Without a shared harness, each cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison\n     8→  logic, the normalization hooks needed for non-deterministic output (timestamps in ts,\n     9→  path ordering in find), and the divergence reporting format. This duplication makes\n    10→  the verification layer fragile: a bug in one cmd/'s harness copy would produce\n    11→  different verification behavior than another cmd/'s copy.\n    12→\n    13→  pkg/testutils is the acceptance gate for the entire pipeline. The mage-claude-orchestrator\n    14→  cannot declare a utility complete until its differential tests pass through this harness.\n    15→\n    16→goals:\n    17→  - G1: Provide a single execution function that runs a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and returns both outputs for comparison.\n    18→  - G2: Provide a comparison function that diffs stdout, stderr, and exit code and reports divergence with the triggering input.\n    19→  - G3: Provide a normalization hook mechanism so individual utilities can strip or replace non-deterministic output fields before comparison.\n    20→  - G4: Integrate with go test so that each cmd/ package can define test cases as data and call the harness from a standard TestXxx function.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Test case data structure\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: |\n    27→          Must define DiffTest as the following named struct in package testutils:\n    28→\n    29→            type DiffTest struct {\n    30→                Name          string                 // subtest name used with t.Run; required\n    31→                Args          []string               // command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n    32→                Stdin         []byte                 // nil = no stdin (both binaries receive EOF immediately)\n    33→                Env           []string               // nil = inherit test process environment with no overrides; non-nil values are KEY=VALUE pairs that override or extend the inherited environment\n    34→                ExitCode      int                    // expected exit code for both binaries\n    35→                Normalize     []func([]byte) []byte  // applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison; nil or empty = no normalization\n    36→                ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte      // optional: path -> expected byte content after execution, for file-output utilities (sponge, cp)\n    37→            }\n    38→      - R1.2: DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately on stdin. An empty non-nil slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but is semantically distinct (open stdin that is immediately closed).\n    39→      - R1.3: DiffTest.Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment unchanged. When non-nil, the slice replaces (does not append to) the process environment for that invocation.\n    40→      - R1.4: Must define type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias in package testutils. The Normalize field in DiffTest uses this element type. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied to stdout or stderr.\n    41→\n    42→  R2:\n    43→    title: Binary execution\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R2.1: |\n    46→          Must provide func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that accepts the path to the Go binary under test (goBinary) and the GNU reference binary (refBinary), then runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...). Both binaries are invoked with identical Args, Stdin, and Env for each test case.\n    47→      - R2.2: Must capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently.\n    48→      - R2.3: Must impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails if either binary exceeds the timeout.\n    49→      - R2.4: Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run.\n    50→      - R2.5: Must support passing a working directory to each binary invocation. When not specified, both binaries run in a per-test temporary directory created by the harness.\n    51→\n    52→  R3:\n    53→    title: Comparison and reporting\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R3.1: Must apply the DiffTest.Normalize function (if non-nil) to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison.\n    56→      - R3.2: Must compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n    57→      - R3.3: Must compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n    58→      - R3.4: Must compare exit codes exactly. A difference must be reported as a test failure.\n    59→      - R3.5: 'Must report divergence with the following information in the failure message: the DiffTest args, the stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference stderr, the Go stderr, and both exit codes.'\n    60→      - R3.6: Must run each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run) so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\n    61→\n    62→  R4:\n    63→    title: Normalization hooks\n    64→    items:\n    65→      - R4.1: Must allow per-DiffTest normalization via the Normalize field. The function receives the raw output bytes and returns the normalized bytes.\n    66→      - R4.2: Must provide a built-in TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder string. This normalizer is used by cmd/ts tests.\n    67→      - R4.3: 'Must allow normalizers to be composed: the harness must accept a slice of Normalize functions applied in order, not a single function. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied.'\n    68→\n    69→  R5:\n    70→    title: File-state comparison\n    71→    items:\n    72→      - R5.1: Must support utilities whose primary output is a file rather than stdout (sponge, cp, mv). For these, DiffTest must allow specifying expected file content after execution alongside expected stdout/stderr.\n    73→      - R5.2: Must compare expected file content byte-for-byte and report divergence in the same format as stdout divergence.\n    74→\n    75→non_goals:\n    76→  - pkg/testutils does not build or compile the Go binaries under test.\n    77→  - pkg/testutils does not install or manage Homebrew reference binaries.\n    78→  - pkg/testutils does not produce test reports beyond standard go test output.\n    79→  - pkg/testutils does not implement retry logic; a failing test fails immediately.\n    80→  - pkg/testutils does not mock or stub the filesystem or OS for unit testing cmd/ packages.\n    81→\n    82→acceptance_criteria:\n    83→  - \"AC1: A cmd/ package can define a []DiffTest slice and call RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) from a TestXxx function with no other setup. RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run(test.Name, ...)) and calls t.Fatal on the first divergence in any subtest.\"\n    84→  - \"AC2: A passing test run produces no output. A failing test prints the triggering args, stdin, and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code.\"\n    85→  - \"AC3: TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., \\\"Feb 19 12:34:56\\\") with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences.\"\n    86→  - \"AC4: A DiffTest with a file expectation field correctly fails when the Go binary writes different content than the reference binary.\"\n    87→  - \"AC5: A DiffTest that exceeds the timeout fails with a clear timeout message rather than hanging the test run.\"\n    88→  - \"AC6: All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    89→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"194482ed-ed27-4cbe-809d-189a6a1053ce"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01EpoLHKWXazFvrwj8kXJyii","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019ZQgYU4YWbh53PLgPKF4rz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5033,"cache_read_input_tokens":21912,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5033},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"16af9d24-5fbc-40de-b50c-b460e45719a8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ZQgYU4YWbh53PLgPKF4rz","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    22→  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    23→\n    24→requirements:\n    25→  R1:\n    26→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    27→    items:\n    28→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    29→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    30→      - R1.3: |\n    31→          Must provide func IsTerminal(w io.Writer) bool that returns true when w is an *os.File whose file descriptor is a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(f.Fd()), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, bytes.Buffer, and any non-*os.File writer. Required by pkg/format to suppress color output when stdout is redirected.\n    32→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    33→\n    34→  R2:\n    35→    title: Extended file metadata\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    38→      - R2.2: |\n    39→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    40→\n    41→            type FileInfo struct {\n    42→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    43→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    44→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    45→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    46→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    47→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    48→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    49→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    50→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    51→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    52→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    53→                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    54→            }\n    55→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    56→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    57→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    58→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    59→\n    60→  R3:\n    61→    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    62→    items:\n    63→      - R3.1: |\n    64→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    65→      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    66→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    67→\n    68→  R4:\n    69→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    70→    items:\n    71→      - R4.1: |\n    72→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    73→      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    74→      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    75→\n    76→non_goals:\n    77→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    78→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    79→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    80→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    81→\n    82→acceptance_criteria:\n    83→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    84→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    85→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    86→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    87→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    88→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    89→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"b7fa6bbc-1e4b-4be2-8f03-abbe886971f5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01EpoLHKWXazFvrwj8kXJyii","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SWMV9bZSidXNuxHBDrkxJE","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5033,"cache_read_input_tokens":21912,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5033},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"4cc38928-2a00-4326-a872-9fefbac09066"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SWMV9bZSidXNuxHBDrkxJE","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd003-format\n     2→title: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n     6→  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n     7→  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n     8→  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n     9→  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n    10→  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n    11→  harder.\n    12→\n    13→  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n    14→  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n    15→  not speculation.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n    19→  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    20→  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Column alignment\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n    27→      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n    28→      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count or east-asian width), not byte count.'\n    29→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n    30→\n    31→  R2:\n    32→    title: ANSI color\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n    35→      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n    36→      - R2.3: |\n    37→          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. It must call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(w io.Writer) bool, which returns true when w is an *os.File whose file descriptor is a terminal (detected via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with TIOCGWINSZ), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-*os.File writers. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n    38→      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n    39→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n    40→\n    41→  R3:\n    42→    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    43→    items:\n    44→      - R3.1: |\n    45→          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n    46→\n    47→            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n    48→                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n    49→            }\n    50→      - R3.2: |\n    51→          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n    52→      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n    53→      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n    54→      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n    55→      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n    56→\n    57→non_goals:\n    58→  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n    59→  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n    60→  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n    61→  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n    62→\n    63→acceptance_criteria:\n    64→  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    65→  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    66→  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    67→  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    68→  - \"AC5: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    69→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"688430cc-edcd-410b-b4c1-b0ae1a8029ed"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_017m1pGpnesNpGGJ9ZwBX2AH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JvUTUs6DdsD8aTyfGuYCJw","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.0-uc001-ls.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6954,"cache_read_input_tokens":26945,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6954},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"4f4595ac-8702-42ee-9862-5ebaac6bee61"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JvUTUs6DdsD8aTyfGuYCJw","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel02.0-uc001-ls\n     2→title: \"List directory contents via ls (core flags)\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go ls binary on a known fixture directory with core flags (default,\n     6→  -1, -l, -a, -A, -d, --color, -h). The differential testing harness runs both the Go binary\n     7→  and the Homebrew reference binary (gls from coreutils) with the same arguments and\n     8→  environment, applies an LsNormalizer to both outputs (replacing mtime columns with a fixed\n     9→  placeholder), and confirms that the normalized outputs are identical. All tests run with\n    10→  --color=never and LC_ALL=C to eliminate ANSI and locale variance.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd008-ls.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n    16→  (prd002-sys, prd003-format) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n    17→  test for cmd/ls core flags.\n    18→\n    19→flow:\n    20→  - id: F1\n    21→    step: |\n    22→      The operator runs the Go ls binary on a fixture directory with known file types,\n    23→      sizes, and permissions. Example: ./bin/ls --color=never testdata/ls-fixture/\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      cmd/ls reads the directory entries, queries terminal width via pkg/sys.TerminalWidth\n    27→      (for default multi-column mode), and obtains file metadata via pkg/sys.Stat for long\n    28→      format. It formats output via pkg/format.Columns (multi-column) or inline formatting\n    29→      (long format with pkg/format.HumanSize for -h).\n    30→  - id: F3\n    31→    step: |\n    32→      cmd/ls writes the formatted listing to stdout. The format depends on the active flags:\n    33→      multi-column (default TTY), single-column (default pipe or -1), or long format (-l).\n    34→  - id: F4\n    35→    step: |\n    36→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/ls and gls with\n    37→      the same arguments, environment (LC_ALL=C, --color=never), and working directory.\n    38→  - id: F5\n    39→    step: |\n    40→      The harness applies the LsNormalizer to both outputs. The LsNormalizer replaces the\n    41→      modification time column in -l output (fields matching \"Mon DD HH:MM\" or \"Mon DD  YYYY\")\n    42→      with the placeholder \"MTIME\". This eliminates wall-clock divergence between runs.\n    43→  - id: F6\n    44→    step: |\n    45→      The harness compares the normalized outputs byte-for-byte. Both binaries must produce\n    46→      identical entry names, sort order, column layout, and field values (permissions,\n    47→      hard-link count, owner, group, size).\n    48→  - id: F7\n    49→    step: |\n    50→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    51→      triggering args, both raw outputs, both normalized outputs, and both exit codes.\n    52→\n    53→touchpoints:\n    54→  - T1: \"cmd/ls — directory listing, format modes, filter flags, color, human sizes (prd008-ls R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7)\"\n    55→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — TerminalWidth, Stat/Lstat, IsTerminal, InstallSIGPIPEHandler, OnTerminalResize (prd002-sys)\"\n    56→  - T3: \"pkg/format — Columns, FileTypeColor, HumanSize (prd003-format)\"\n    57→  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, LsNormalizer, binary execution and comparison\"\n    58→\n    59→success_criteria:\n    60→  - id: S1\n    61→    criterion: cmd/ls compiles and ./bin/ls exists after mage build.\n    62→  - id: S2\n    63→    criterion: |\n    64→      Default mode with stdout redirected (not a TTY) produces single-column output with\n    65→      one entry per line, matching gls output on the same fixture.\n    66→  - id: S3\n    67→    criterion: |\n    68→      -l long format produces identical output to gls -l after MTIME normalization on the\n    69→      same fixture directory.\n    70→  - id: S4\n    71→    criterion: |\n    72→      -a includes dotfiles (including . and ..) and matches gls -a output on a fixture\n    73→      containing dotfiles.\n    74→  - id: S5\n    75→    criterion: |\n    76→      --color=never produces output with no ANSI escape sequences, matching gls --color=never\n    77→      byte-for-byte.\n    78→  - id: S6\n    79→    criterion: |\n    80→      -lh produces human-readable file sizes that match gls -lh on files with known sizes.\n    81→  - id: S7\n    82→    criterion: |\n    83→      The differential test passes for all core flags: default (single-col redirect),\n    84→      -1, -l, -lh, -a, -A, -d, --color=never. All tests use LC_ALL=C and --color=never\n    85→      (except color-specific tests).\n    86→\n    87→out_of_scope:\n    88→  - Default multi-column mode on a real TTY is difficult to test deterministically in the\n    89→    harness; we test -C explicitly in the ls extended use case (rel02.1).\n    90→  - Owner/group name normalization is not applied; tests use a fixture where files are owned\n    91→    by the test runner's UID/GID, ensuring consistency.\n    92→  - SIGWINCH behavior is specified in prd008-ls R7.2 but is not directly testable in the\n    93→    differential harness.\n    94→\n    95→test_suite: test-rel02.0\n    96→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rr3Nc2sNzdyNsNfUbKv85p","session_id":"f6586651-9e84-4147-a394-d5fc5f9feda5","uuid":"4cefebb0-c6c2-4602-a49b-855f1fada040"}
